<Prose title="Privacy" description="Your files stay in a folder on your disk. No account, no telemetry. Chat, Rewrite and Connected programs are apps that do nothing until you switch them on. Writ contacts GitHub to check for updates and to download one you install." path="/privacy/" lead="Where your files live, and what leaves the machine. Reviewed 2026-09-24.">
  <h2>Your files</h2>
  <p>
    Writ opens any text file on your disk and edits it where it is. It also keeps a folder of its own,
    <code>~/Writ</code> unless you moved it; <code>Settings &rarr; Files &rarr; Folder</code> shows the path. New
    files and anything you pipe into the <code>writ</code> command land there, and search covers every file in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Writ's data folder</h2>
  <p>
    Writ keeps its own records in <code>~/.writ</code>, which <code>Settings &rarr; Advanced</code> also shows. Among
    them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>writ.db</code> holds the search index, which stores the text of each file in the folder so search can
      find it, along with the links, tags and properties read from those files, tab order and window state. It also
      holds a recovery snapshot of the text in your open tabs up to 5&nbsp;MB each, including text you typed that had not reached its
      file when Writ quit.
    </li>
    <li><code>config.toml</code> holds your settings. It holds no API key.</li>
    <li>
      <code>history/</code> and <code>history.db</code> hold earlier versions of the files in the folder, so a bad
      edit or a program's write can be undone. Writ keeps a version each time a file is saved, for up to 30 days or
      200 versions per file, and no more than 250&nbsp;MB in all. A file over 2&nbsp;MB is not versioned.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>chats/</code> holds one file per conversation: your messages, the replies, the edits a reply proposed,
      and the paths and sizes of attached files. It never holds an attached file's text or a key.
    </li>
    <li><code>activity.jsonl</code> lists what connected programs and chat did with your files, without their text.</li>
    <li><code>logs/</code> records what the app did and the paths it opened. Logs rotate daily and are kept.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of this is written into your folder, so a sync client never carries it.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app does on the network</h2>
  <p>
    Until you switch on Chat or Rewrite, Writ makes one kind of outbound request: it fetches a release manifest from
    GitHub and compares versions on your machine. It does this on its own shortly after launch, at most once a day,
    and whenever you ask in <code>Settings &rarr; Updates</code>. Turn the automatic check off with
    <code>Check for updates automatically</code>. Installing an update downloads the release from GitHub and checks it
    against a signing key built into the app.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spell check runs against a dictionary bundled in the app. The preview pane runs under a fixed
    content-security-policy (<code>default-src 'none'</code>, <code>connect-src 'none'</code>), so rendered HTML makes
    no network calls. Writ sends no crash reports, usage data or telemetry.
  </p>

  <h2>The AI connection</h2>
  <p>
    Chat and Rewrite are apps you switch on in <code>Settings &rarr; Apps</code>. While either is on, the same
    section holds the connection they share: provider, address, API key and model. Ollama and LM Studio run on your
    machine and need no key, so a request to either stays there.
  </p>
  <p>
    Point the connection at a hosted provider and Writ asks once, naming the host, before it sends that host
    anything. The answer is recorded for that host alone, so allowing one provider does not cover another or a
    hand-edited address. An address that is neither on this machine nor <code>https</code> is refused before any
    bytes leave.
  </p>
  <p>
    To show whether the provider is reachable, Writ requests its model list. That request carries your API key and
    no text, and goes to a hosted provider only after you allowed it. Settings also asks Ollama and LM Studio on this
    machine whether they are running; that request carries nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Connecting OpenRouter opens openrouter.ai in your browser. Writ listens on this machine for the one answer that
    browser sends back, then exchanges it for a key and stores the key like one you typed. No port is open at any
    other time.
  </p>

  <h2>Rewrite</h2>
  <p>
    A rewrite sends the selected text, the instruction for the action you picked, and the model id. With nothing
    selected, Writ asks before sending the whole file.
  </p>

  <h2>Chat</h2>
  <p>
    A chat request carries a fixed instruction, the files attached to the conversation, the conversation so far and
    the model id. The pane attaches the file in front of you and shows it as a chip you can remove; you add others
    with <code>@</code> or by attaching a folder. Any file open in the editor can be attached, including one outside
    your folder, which is sent under its parent folder and file name, never its full path. Nothing that is not
    attached is read for a request.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chat writes a file only when you choose Apply on a proposed edit, and no setting applies one for you. If the file
    changed after the edit was proposed, Writ refuses to apply it. For a file in your folder, the proposed text is
    saved beside it as a dated copy; for a file outside it, the proposed text stays in the conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>Connected programs</h2>
  <p>
    Writ ships an MCP server, <code>writ mcp</code>, so a program you choose can work with the files in your folder.
    Connected programs is an app you switch on in <code>Settings &rarr; Apps</code>, which also shows the command to
    give the program. The program starts the server itself and talks to it over that process's input and output.
    Writ opens no port, and the server makes no network request. Where that program sends what it reads is up to
    that program.
  </p>
  <p>
    Switching the app on approves nobody. A program's first call is refused and waits in Settings until you approve
    that program. Reading and writing are approved separately; approving reading never grants writing. Either can be
    withdrawn in Settings or in <code>config.toml</code> and takes effect on the next call.
  </p>
  <p>
    With reading approved, a program has eight tools: list the folder's Markdown files, search the folder, read a
    file, read a file's links, backlinks, properties and tags, and list the folder's tags. With writing approved, it
    has three: replace a file's text, create a file, and rename one. No tool deletes a file. A write that passes the
    version the program read is refused if the file changed since, and the program's text is saved beside the file.
    A file outside your folder is out of reach.
  </p>

  <h2>The activity list</h2>
  <p>
    Every call from a program, whether it was allowed, refused or left waiting, and every chat edit you applied or
    discarded, is appended to <code>activity.jsonl</code>. A record names the program or chat, the tool, the file,
    the decision and, for a write, a byte count, never the text. The file stays on your disk and is capped at 5&nbsp;MB with one
    older generation kept. While Connected programs is on, open it from
    <code>Settings &rarr; Apps &rarr; Connected programs &rarr; Recent activity</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Keys</h2>
  <p>
    On macOS and Windows, Writ stores an API key in the system keychain under the service <code>com.writ.ai</code>.
    Where the keychain is unavailable, Writ holds the key in memory for the session and warns that you will enter it
    again next time. A key is never written to <code>config.toml</code>, the database or a log.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site does</h2>
  <p>
    This site is a static export served from GitHub Pages, and it serves its own font. It counts page views with a
    self-hosted Umami script from stats.ibrahemid.com, which it tells to honor Do Not Track. It sets no cookies and
    embeds no third-party widgets.
  </p>

  <h2>Backup and deletion</h2>
  <p>
    Back up your folder and any file you keep elsewhere; the files are the only copy of your text. Writ can rebuild
    the index in <code>~/.writ</code> from the folder. Nothing else in <code>~/.writ</code> can be rebuilt:
    settings, earlier versions, conversations and the activity list.
  </p>
  <p>
    Delete <code>~/.writ</code> to return the app to a fresh install. Your folder and every file you opened elsewhere
    stay untouched. An API key is not in that directory: clear it from your keychain, or from
    <code>Settings &rarr; Apps</code> while Chat or Rewrite is on.
  </p>

  <h2>Source of truth</h2>
  <p>
    Everything above is in the repository,
    <a href="https://github.com/ibrahemid/writ" rel="noopener" target="_blank">github.com/ibrahemid/writ</a>, with
    the decisions behind it in
    <a href="https://github.com/ibrahemid/writ/blob/main/docs/adr/031-the-ai-harness-and-what-leaves-the-machine.md" rel="noopener" target="_blank">ADR-031</a>
    and the checks in
    <a href="https://github.com/ibrahemid/writ/blob/main/docs/threat-model.md" rel="noopener" target="_blank">the threat model</a>.
    If you find a network call this page does not describe, file an issue and it will be treated as a bug.
  </p>
</Prose>
